A newsreader that doesn't give you the option to mask and/or toggle
specified headers, thus forcing the body to start "one-third to
one-half" of the way down the screen, is poor. 

This has nothing to do with the placement of headers, but instead with
which and how many are displayed.  They should of course appear before
the body, but only the ones you choose.  

I read newsgroups in a 24-line terminal window, yet I don't have this
problem.  I use a good newsreader, so I only see the headers I choose
and the body starts about 7 lines down.   

There's nothing wrong with the "I prefer to use a newsreader that's
kinda crappy" argument, per se.  In this case, I support it, since the
behavior inconveniencing these folks is improper in the general case.  I
just don't see how Martin's case fits into anything other than the
"crappy newsreader" argument.  

